---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RefinanceCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/RefinanceCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="refinance-calculator">
  <RefinanceCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the refinance calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The refinance calculator works out two payments with the level-payment formula
      <code>M = P × i ÷ (1 − (1 + i)^−n)</code>: one for the loan you have (balance, rate and months left) and one
      for the new loan (balance plus any closing costs you add to it, the new rate and term). The monthly saving is
      the difference. The break-even month is <code>closing costs ÷ monthly saving</code>, rounded up.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lifetime difference adds up every payment left on the current loan and compares it with every payment on
      the new loan plus the closing costs paid in cash. The chart shows both running totals year by year.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 300,000 balance at 7.25% with 27 years left has a payment of 2,112.58. A new 30-year loan at 6% has a payment
      of 1,798.65, so you save 313.93 a month. With 6,000 of closing costs paid in cash, the saving pays them back in
      20 months. Over the life of both loans, refinancing costs 30,962.50 less, even with three more years of payments.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 15-year loan at 5.5% on the same balance raises the payment to 2,451.25, but the total paid falls by about
      237,000.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Both loans have fixed rates and the result covers principal and interest only. Taxes, insurance and PMI are
      left out. If you plan to sell or refinance again before the break-even month, the refinance costs more than it
      saves.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
